WEBVTT

1
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.219
Thorin: How's it going, man?

2
00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.190
Thorin: Oh, you're muted.

3
00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.360
Thorin: Let me turn on my light, it's here somewhere.

4
00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:59.670
Grygum: Whoa, whoa?

5
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.050
Thorin: Hello!

6
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.650
Thorin: You're still muted, man.

7
00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:21.689
Daz: Yeah, Zoom is being done. It won't… I found a way to unmute myself, but it wouldn't let me, it wouldn't let me bring my… like, I was trying to make sure I was on the latest version and find where settings are now, and I can't find it, but in addition to that, it was, once I brought that other window up, it, like.

8
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:27.210
Daz: yeeted this whole thing up into a little bitty window in the upper right-hand corner of my screen that I couldn't see.

9
00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.060
Daz: Anyway.

10
00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.320
Thorin: I don't use Zoom every day anymore, I, like, most of our company runs on Hangouts, and so, like…

11
00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:35.970
Thorin: I get…

12
00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:36.710
Grygum: Jeez.

13
00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:41.230
Grygum: I didn't realize Hangouts was still around.

14
00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:44.409
Thorin: Or Google Meet or whatever, I don't know, it's the same damn thing.

15
00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:45.110
Daz: Yeah.

16
00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:48.929
Thorin: Google Talk, that's what I… that's what I worked on, that's what we called it, I don't know.

17
00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:53.990
Grygum: There's, there's one, one person I, I talked to over,

18
00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:58.399
Grygum: Google… whatever it's called, and that's my sister in Japan.

19
00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.019
Grygum: I… I don't know why, but it seems to be what we settled on.

20
00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:11.429
Daz: I, I use it for a science… science fiction book club month thing. We all get on Google Meet for that.

21
00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.860
Daz: And then, at work, I use WebEx.

22
00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:16.199
Daz: Because I work for.

23
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:16.890
Grygum: Well…

24
00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:17.810
Thorin: I'm sorry.

25
00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:18.300
Grygum: Well, it.

26
00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.160
Thorin: WebEx is, like, literally the worst.

27
00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.469
Daz: It's gotten a lot better, but it's still not great. It's better than Teams.

28
00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:25.770
Thorin: Oh, teams…

29
00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:36.349
Thorin: See, I log into Teams, and I'm like, there's always something fucked up with, like, Microsoft accounts and stuff, and I can never figure it out. So I always give up, and I'm just like, just do the web thing, and don't bother logging in.

30
00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:37.310
Daz: Right.

31
00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:51.690
Grygum: See, I always think of this radio program committee meeting where somebody had written this paper that was all about, you know, here's this great video conferencing product we could make.

32
00:03:51.690 --> 00:04:09.059
Grygum: And the person talking about the paper listed off all the lists of features that they wanted. And then somebody else in the program committee stood up and said, you know, WebEx actually has all those features, we just don't pay for them.

33
00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:14.460
Grygum: And we shut down the paper like that.

34
00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:16.260
Grygum: I really…

35
00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:27.359
Daz: I really wish you could do it. I will say one thing about WebEx that it does bet… probably better than Zoom. It works very well if you have a very shitty internet connection.

36
00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:34.329
Daz: It will just drop the frame rate to, like, 3 frames a second, and keep the audio going.

37
00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:35.250
Thorin: Oh, yeah.

38
00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:36.269
Daz: It won't just…

39
00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:37.770
Thorin: Prioritize audio, yeah.

40
00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:38.370
Daz: Yeah.

41
00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:41.010
Thorin: Ow.

42
00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:44.190
Thorin: Aye, aye, aye.

43
00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.930
Thorin: How y'all doing? Anything new going on?

44
00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:48.430
Daz: My, my whole company.

45
00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:48.960
Grygum: Ugh.

46
00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.769
Daz: New York, so…

47
00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:58.859
Daz: I used to work… it's a… you guys… do you guys remember Tom Gillis, the GM for NSX and security?

48
00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:00.810
Thorin: Yeah.

49
00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:02.779
Grygum: Yeah, yeah, I, I, I do.

50
00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:12.109
Daz: I… I worked for Craig Connors, who was, like, the guy that came in with BellaCloud. I worked for him under Tom, and Tom…

51
00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:21.810
Daz: just got, switched out as the GM for that group, because they did a big reorg under all of the product stuff, and is going to be A'lai's new CTO.

52
00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:27.230
Thorin: What… I wonder, like, what does the job of a CTO look like at a company?

53
00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:34.410
Daz: So, I think for… I mean, Tom's technical in that, like, early in his career, he wrote, like, guidance software for the Patriot missiles.

54
00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:39.859
Daz: But, like, hasn't really done… like, then he went into the business side of things and stayed in the business side the whole time.

55
00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:46.400
Daz: So I think he's in more of the chief talking officer side, like, where he's, you know…

56
00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:50.100
Thorin: to CTOs, and, like, they all pretend that they used to be engineers.

57
00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:51.160
Daz: Yeah.

58
00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:53.970
Daz: Yeah, so there's some of that going on, but…

59
00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:58.580
Thorin: My favorite was every time that, that,

60
00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:00.999
Thorin: God, I'm blanking on his name!

61
00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:04.680
Thorin: like, old CEO of VMware…

62
00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:06.310
Daz: Grygum?

63
00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:07.030
Thorin: What's that?

64
00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:08.170
Daz: Grygum?

65
00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:13.679
Thorin: No, no, What's that? No, the one that bought our company, I'm blanking on his name.

66
00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:14.600
GM: Hawk 10?

67
00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:18.739
Thorin: No, the one before that. They bought our company as in Heptio.

68
00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:21.080
Grygum: Gelsinger?

69
00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:39.709
Thorin: Yeah, yeah. So every time Pat would talk, he would love to talk about how he's still a real engineer, and we'd all nod along, like, yeah, dude, you're still, like, you know, you're super technical still, don't worry, like… and he would start, like, he'd be talking software, he's like, yeah, I really, you know, I would talk speeds and feeds, I'm like, that's not a thing in software. We don't, we don't do speeds and feeds and software.

70
00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:44.999
Grygum: You know, at my, at my startup at Nassera… Those of us who work.

71
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.749
GM: on speeds and feeds and software raise a hand in objection.

72
00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:49.980
Grygum: We both agree.

73
00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:53.179
GM: Wait, wait. Just for the record…

74
00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:55.619
Thorin: CNC control software? I mean, like…

75
00:06:56.250 --> 00:07:00.959
GM: But we… some of us did worry about speeds and feats, but we will… we will digress.

76
00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:04.600
Daz: I wrote… I wrote the very first ever…

77
00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:13.689
Daz: data plane for a network service device, basically a firewall, that was embedded in a modular switch.

78
00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.770
Daz: And that, you very much had to worry about speeds and feeds.

79
00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:19.500
Thorin: Well, what do speeds and feeds mean in that context?

80
00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:29.499
Daz: Basically, I have a backplane that can throw 256 gigabits at me, and I'm trying to keep up, and all I can actually do is about a gigabit.

81
00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:33.799
Thorin: No, speeds and feeds, you're talking about CNC, where you actually have things spinning, which is the speed.

82
00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:34.930
Daz: Oh, right, right, right.

83
00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:36.170
Thorin: Speed is, like, how fast you move.

84
00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:36.799
Daz: move it across the board.

85
00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:37.520
Thorin: part.

86
00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:40.210
Daz: Yeah, people stole it in the network world.

87
00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:40.950
Thorin: Oh, okay.

88
00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:46.020
Daz: It feeds to mean, like, bandwidth and, and, like, yeah.

89
00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:46.430
GM: Yeah.

90
00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:51.690
Daz: So, that had moved from machine shops into networking parlance.

91
00:07:51.960 --> 00:08:02.059
GM: Yes, that's literally where I stumbled across it as well, where I worked on a network packet thing that shipped, the gigabit network speeds on a Celeron.

92
00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:02.680
Thorin: Okay.

93
00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:07.050
Thorin: Yeah, and all the networking people got pissed off that we took over the term control plane.

94
00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:10.470
GM: Well, we give them credit.

95
00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:14.860
Grygum: Oh,

96
00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:15.930
GM: Yes.

97
00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:21.030
Grygum: GM, can you accept my friend request on Discord so I can add some DMs with.

98
00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:22.790
GM: Oh, which one, who are you?

99
00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:28.150
Grygum: My, Woggle Bug, that's my, my discard name.

100
00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:30.570
GM: Why do I not have you?

101
00:08:30.940 --> 00:08:31.790
Grygum: I don't know.

102
00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:33.159
Grygum: I don't see…

103
00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:35.339
Thorin: Why is 1Password not letting me log in?

104
00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:39.999
Grygum: Discord basically hides the friend requests, so you have to dig a bit to even find them.

105
00:08:40.679 --> 00:08:42.159
GM: That's pending.

106
00:08:44.519 --> 00:08:47.959
GM: We weren't fun… we weren't friends until today. How sad.

107
00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:54.259
Grygum: Well, it's almost meaningless on Discord, except for DMing people who you aren't in a common server with, something like that.

108
00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:54.950
GM: Yes.

109
00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:57.600
GM: It's always very fascinating.

110
00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:03.709
Thorin: So my computer was off long enough that 1Password needed to be restarted twice to update multiple times.

111
00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.000
Grygum: That would make it 2Password.

112
00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:06.940
Thorin: I know, right?

113
00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:08.000
Daz: Just.

114
00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:10.369
GM: Just for the… just for the, the record.

115
00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:15.349
GM: Just for the record, Daz, you never worked at a startup? I don't think I quite understood that.

116
00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:18.640
Daz: So, I… I worked at a very small company.

117
00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:20.820
Daz: So, I did a startup-y thing.

118
00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:39.620
Daz: When I was at UT, and I was getting my electrical engineering degree, there was this entrepreneur guy that has, like, a serial entrepreneur, he starts a bunch of different companies. He… this is… I'm gonna try to condense this story as small as possible. He was at IBM selling them, process equipment for a company he repped.

119
00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:58.439
Daz: And they had this laser that had all these… this laser chassis thing, and it had all these stickers on it saying, do not use decommissioning body. And he asked what happened. Somebody didn't follow the rules, and they overrode the thing, and it caused a safety incident, and it was, like, a nitty and gag 100-watt laser with, like, a 4-axis

120
00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:00.440
Daz: robot.

121
00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:01.870
Thorin: Now we're talking speeds and feeds.

122
00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:11.890
Daz: Now we're talking speed to speed. And he bought it from them. He's like, well, what are you guys gonna do with it? And they're like, we're just gonna decommission to get out of here. And, like, he bought it from them for cheap. And then he paid to get it moved.

123
00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:17.349
Daz: And then he had this bright idea, because he was all involved in surface mount stuff, of, like, when you build…

124
00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:19.340
Grygum: 100-watt lasers are very bright.

125
00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:27.830
Daz: Yes, very bright. And so, when you build stencils for, for, like, squeegeeing solder paste onto a surface mount.

126
00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:28.910
Daz: board.

127
00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:45.660
Daz: You have to have these little voids, and you squeegee over the voids, and the way that that's typically done is they do this, like, photo thing, and then drop acid on it, and it eats in from both sides, and you end up with something that looks like an hourglass, and then you squeegee through the hourglass, and the middle of the hourglass.

128
00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:49.919
Daz: Causes, like, volume problems, because sometimes it takes the top half

129
00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:55.459
Daz: back up with it. And he's like, hey, if we shoot it from a laser and we make it more like a trapezoid… So anyway.

130
00:10:55.940 --> 00:11:15.759
Daz: Cutting this to a much shorter conversation, while I was in school, I programmed the thing, bought a bunch of, like, various different kind of nickel-encoded steel for the… which is what they use for the stencils, because they have to be squeegeed a bunch, and started firing at steel with this laser and trying to figure things out with microscopes… Do you know that steel is reflective?

131
00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:27.889
Daz: Yeah, yeah, and I had the goggles on, like Back to the Future. Anyway, did all that stuff, and then quickly realized that Nidium Yag is not the right type of laser to drill steel.

132
00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:50.370
Daz: and told him that I, like, unfortunately, I filled this out, and he's like, oh, that's okay, I kind of thought this might go sideways, so I already talked to Texas State Technical College, and I had an independent appraiser come in and appraise the laser, and with the tax write-off I'm going to get from donating it to the college, I'm going to end up making, like, $40,000, and that was plenty to pay you part-time to figure out if we could actually turn this into a business. And so he donated it off.

133
00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:57.120
Daz: And then he's like, oh, by the way, I've decided I want to sell this other business, because it's taking a lot of my time, and I want to go fishing.

134
00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:09.120
Daz: Will you come… will you come computerize the business and run it for me? And then I'll tell them that you're the general manager, and they don't need me, and I'll sell the business, and you can run it. And I said…

135
00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:11.820
Daz: I said, okay. And so I did that.

136
00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:18.860
GM: Wait a second, wait a second, wait a second, you did not work at a startup, and yet had several quintessential Silicon Valley startup stories.

137
00:12:21.390 --> 00:12:21.960
Daz: Awesome.

138
00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:28.219
GM: Like, I mean, all I'm waiting for here and now is to find out that there was a safe, you know,

139
00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:44.650
Daz: So anyway, took that job, ran the comp- computerized the company, this is Windows for Workgroups 311, and nettastic over 9,600 bod modems. I'm just dating myself. Computerized it, got all this going.

140
00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:59.310
Daz: Ran the business, they… he did, true to his word, sold the business. The new guys came in, my… they were… this is back when investment banking firms would, like, buy up a bunch of mom-and-pops that were the same, and then try to package them and IPO them, and… and profit.

141
00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:00.659
Thorin: Private equity, okay, yeah.

142
00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:13.819
Daz: Yeah, this is before they were private equity. Anyway, so, I did all that. They loved the way I was running mine, so then I ended up running all of operations, like, west of the Mississippi, and hated it, because I wanted to do nerd stuff.

143
00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:29.860
Daz: And, like, I had all these factory workers, and, like, I was like, no! So I took, like, a 70% pay cut and went to become a contractor for the Air Force Information Warfare Center, because hacking sounded cool, and I wanted to go do that. Moved to San Antonio, and restarted my career.

144
00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:50.149
Daz: Met a bunch of people, some of which were the people that founded, Wheel Group, which was an IDS that got acquired by, A'lai. So then when I wanted to move back to Austin, I called some of them, and they're like, yeah, you can come work for A'lai. And then I went to A'lai, Juniper, VMware, back to A'lai.

145
00:13:50.150 --> 00:13:57.109
Grygum: Oh, that's funny. Did you know Justin Pettit? I think he might have worked for William. Yeah, I knew Justin. Yeah, yeah, you knew Justin, okay, yeah.

146
00:13:57.600 --> 00:13:58.320
Grygum: he was…

147
00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:12.999
Thorin: But this is why I tell people when they're, like, early in their career, I'm like, if all you do is chase money, you're gonna end up in a place that you hate, right? At some point, you gotta figure out what do you want to do, because, like, that job that you landed in sounds like one of those jobs where I'm sure it could pay well, and it's pure hell.

148
00:14:13.310 --> 00:14:16.729
Daz: Yeah, it paid amazingly well for a…

149
00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:22.730
Daz: what was I, the 22-year-old or something? Because I went to school early. Like, I graduated…

150
00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:27.809
Daz: I was… yeah, I was… so I'd have been 22. Yeah, I was 22, and I had that job.

151
00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:28.690
Daz: I mean…

152
00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:34.329
GM: I just remembered I have to send a note to Mark Johnson to tell him that he's not the only high school dropout I knew at VMware.

153
00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:40.029
GM: This is a rubbing gag.

154
00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:42.050
Daz: He doesn't want everybody to know, GM.

155
00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:44.039
GM: I won't tell him. I won't tell him.

156
00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:52.600
GM: Hold on! I will tell him that he was not… actually, I'll even make it worse, he was not the only distinguished engineer who was a high school dropout.

157
00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:54.700
Zalthir: True?

158
00:14:56.090 --> 00:15:07.649
Daz: I find that a lot of people who are very bright get very bored with high school very easily. I may have actually dropped out of high school if I hadn't found this… Texas had this program where you could

159
00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:11.420
Daz: Do your junior and your senior year of high school at university.

160
00:15:11.530 --> 00:15:15.129
Daz: And so my high school diploma is from the University of North Texas.

161
00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:16.680
Zalthir: Oh, that's cool.

162
00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:29.520
GM: Alright, as much as I would enjoy having this conversation to the wee hours of the morning, I think… and as much as I feel my son would then say that we're like that episode of The Simpsons…

163
00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:32.009
Daz: So, I have a question, though, before you get going.

164
00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:32.880
GM: Shoot.

165
00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.330
Daz: Have I worked at a startup?

166
00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:40.250
GM: not a Silicon Valley venture-funded startup.

167
00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:44.610
Daz: This is why I said no, but I kind of have a…

168
00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:46.839
Thorin: And I haven't worked at a startup either.

169
00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:48.770
GM: Yeah, you did, you worked at Heptio.

170
00:15:48.770 --> 00:15:50.770
Thorin: Yeah, we were not a Silicon Valley company.

171
00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:54.730
GM: It was a… it's a… you know, but if you read the thing I wrote.

172
00:15:54.730 --> 00:15:57.020
Thorin: Silicon Valley's the state of mind.

173
00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:03.380
GM: Valleys of mood, a coffee shop! A state of mind! A geography!

174
00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:09.600
GM: Alright.

175
00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:10.640
GM: Alright.

176
00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:11.719
Daz: Sorry for the segue.

177
00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:14.429
GM: No, dude, that was actually worth the price of admission.

178
00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:19.240
GM: Alley… okay, so, just to remind you what happened, Alley…

179
00:16:20.180 --> 00:16:35.039
GM: Good morning. Well, they told the surviving thug to ignore the cleric and go after Daz, shouting that the wizard had the key, though in truth, Grygum quietly held both the key and the gem. Daz conjured a massive explosion of fireball… of fire that engulfed the surviving thug.

180
00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:50.710
GM: And the successful detonation sent shockwaves of a different kind through all of Candlekeep. Bells rang, screams echoed, and scholars scrambled for their notebooks, because the fireball's very existence proved that the library's legendary magical wards had been broken.

181
00:16:50.840 --> 00:17:07.079
